import sys
P=sys.argv[1]
t=open(P,encoding="utf8").read()
start="- **A new code is admitted before it is checked.** A daemon switches only to a digest that another origin attests"
end="the digest changes with every file that arrives, so a partial change set is never switched to and the decision is made again for each state."
a=t.index(start); b=t.index(end,a)+len(end)
assert t.count(start)==1
new='''- **A new code is admitted before it is checked, and the fleet can never wait on itself (`🚦️canary`).** The rule is the pure function `decideCanaryV1` (schema `🚦️canary/🧬️schema/🔣️.json`, language-agnostic fixture with 42 decisions and 7 tracker sequences, independent Python oracle `🐍️.py`); `admitCodeV1` feeds it the attestations, the refusals, the store snapshots and the retirements of the store. In this order: (1) the code that runs is admitted; (2) a digest that any origin **refused** (`origins/<origin>/refused.json`, schema `Refusals`, newest 64) never passes; (3) a digest with a healthy **store snapshot** passes; (4) a digest that another origin runs (fresh attestation, sync on, no fallback) passes for a **follower** only after that canary has stayed healthy for 60 s as seen by this daemon (`createCanaryTrackerV1`: the time starts at the first reading and is kept while readings follow each other within 60 s), until then `waiting: X runs this code and has to stay healthy for N s more`; (5) a digest whose changed files were all published by this origin (**author**, `authoredCodeV1`, asked again after 5, 10, … 60 s while false) passes; (6) otherwise nobody has proven it and the candidates go **in turn**: the candidates are this origin and every other fresh (60 s), syncing, non-fallback, non-retired origin of the group, the group server `<group>-server` first and the rest by ascending id; after **2 minutes** (counted from the first time this daemon was asked about the digest, a new digest starts again) the first candidate goes, each further one **5 minutes** later (a canary needs jitter, self-check and boot), and only if no earlier candidate made the code run in between; a silent server drops out of the order by itself. The waiting text says who goes first and in how many seconds, in English and German. Every candidate still runs its own self-check before it switches, and a canary that fails stops the fleet: a failed self-check (not a timeout or cancellation of the check, which say something about the machine) publishes a refusal of the digest, so does a daemon that runs a fallback code because the worktree code did not start (it refuses the worktree digest, for itself in `history.json` and for the fleet), and so does a daemon whose predecessor of the same code was ended by the watchdog within 30 minutes of that code's first run (it records the refusal and steps aside; the boot stage then starts the last sealed code, because a refused digest is a rejected start). Nobody switches to a refused digest again; a changed file is a new digest. This replaces the old rule "go first only if every file was written here, else wait until another origin runs it", which deadlocked as soon as the files of a change set were written on several machines (2026-11-14: all eight origins waiting for 4.5 hours).'''
t=t[:a]+new+t[b:]
marker="- **A blocked event loop is visible and bounded.**"
assert t.count(marker)==1
extra='''- **A blocked apply pass backs off, and a lost pack event cannot stall the fleet.** Deletions under `.git/objects` wait until every pack index of the manifest has its pack (so a repack never leaves old packs gone and the new one missing). An index whose pack the manifest does not hold as a put and that is older than ten minutes is an orphan and holds nothing back; before, one lost pack event (2026-11-14: `pack-afe8…idx` published at 02:03, its pack never) kept 52 deletions queued on every origin, which kept the incoming queue non-empty and with it every **checkpoint** (checkpoints are taken only with an empty queue, so the common checkpoint was 3.6 hours old). While a pass is blocked on this and the queue has not changed, the next pass waits 0.5 s, then twice as long up to 30 s (`pack-incomplete` was counted 6 times a second as the failure kind `blocked`); a change of the queue (an event arrives, a file is applied) runs it at once.
'''
t=t.replace(marker,extra+marker)
open(P,"w",encoding="utf8").write(t)
print("ok")
t=open(P,encoding="utf8").read()
row="| `🩺️attest` | What a worker states about itself"
assert t.count(row)==1
t=t.replace(row,"| `🚦️canary` | Who may switch to new code and in which order (`decideCanaryV1`, the canary tracker, the refusal records `origins/<origin>/refused.json`); see \"Boot that cannot be bricked\". |\n"+row)
old="and reports it as a `litter` progress count."
assert t.count(old)==1
t=t.replace(old,old+" The cause is the extended attribute `com.apple.provenance` that the kernel adds to every file a process writes: an NFS client without named attributes stores it as a 4096-byte `._<name>` next to the file, so no mount option or `COPYFILE_DISABLE` avoids it (a 4096-byte `._` file of the live store carries exactly that attribute). Readers ignore them (`listOriginsV1` takes directories with an origin-id name, the flat heads and the segment lists match patterns, the attestation readers open exact names; tested), and the sweep also covers `v1/fleet` and `v1/bootstrap` at any depth and the root `v1` (there only a `._<name>` whose `<name>` exists next to it counts); it runs inside `compaction --apply`, so it does nothing while automatic compaction is off (3411 files on 2026-11-14, 1998 of them under `blobs`).")
open(P,"w",encoding="utf8").write(t)
